From: Domingo Alvarez Duarte <mingo@Domingos-MacBook-Air.local>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 13:21:50 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/8] perf(generate): expand the extras pattern once instead of
 once per token

`expand_tokens` built one NFA per token, each of the form `sep* token`, so
a grammar with N tokens carried N copies of its separator pattern. Since
`sep* a | sep* b` is equivalent to `sep* (a | b)`, the copies are
redundant, and they are what makes lex-state closure expensive for large
grammars: the states reachable from any start state include every token,
so the per-token copies multiply the live set by N.

Build a second NFA in which token bodies stay private, the separator is
expanded once, and the separator's exit fans out through a binary split
tree ("hub") over the bodies. Each token gets a `shared_start`: the shared
separator entry state, or its own body if it is an immediate token, which
is never preceded by a separator. `shared_owner` records which token, if
any, owns each state, so the lex table builder can drop states whose token
is not valid in the parse state being built; shared states are always live.

`TokenConflictMap` keeps using the per-token NFA: it walks two automata in
lockstep and needs the copies to compare two tokens in isolation.

Lexing the PostgreSQL 16 grammar (1195 rules, 489 tokens): 14.1k -> 3.4k
lex states, `build_lex_table` >25s -> 0.5s, peak RSS 942MB -> 87MB.
